From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000026 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cennac <cennac@163.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 15:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] broadcom: disable controller LPM on AGIBOT AP6275P

The AP6275P initializes correctly but stops responding to HCI commands after
the controller enters UART low-power mode. The Rockchip kernel exposes the
btwrite wake node, but the Android HCI transport does not assert BT_WAKE before
the first discovery command. Manually asserting btwrite makes the same legacy
scan command complete without a Bluetooth process restart.

Keep controller sleep disabled for the AGIBOT product. This follows the
existing CYW car-product workaround while limiting the behavior to MB0002.
---
 src/hardware.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/hardware.c b/src/hardware.c
index 1589fae..a564c1b 100644
--- a/src/hardware.c
+++ b/src/hardware.c
@@ -1327,6 +1327,8 @@ uint8_t hw_lpm_enable(uint8_t turn_on)
     HC_BT_HDR  *p_buf = NULL;
     uint8_t     *p;
     uint8_t     ret = FALSE;
+    char        product_device[PROPERTY_VALUE_MAX] = {0};
+    bool        is_agibot_mb0002;
 
     if (bt_vendor_cbacks)
         p_buf = (HC_BT_HDR *) bt_vendor_cbacks->alloc(BT_HC_HDR_SIZE + \
@@ -1342,6 +1344,12 @@ uint8_t hw_lpm_enable(uint8_t turn_on)
 
         if (turn_on)
         {
+            property_get("ro.product.device", product_device, "");
+            is_agibot_mb0002 = !strcmp(product_device, "agibot_mb0002");
+            if (is_agibot_mb0002) {
+                lpm_param.sleep_mode = 0;
+                ALOGW("AGIBOT AP6275P: controller low-power mode disabled");
+            }
             // @Rockchip car fix
             if (wifi_type[0] == 0)
                 check_wifi_chip_type_string(wifi_type);
-- 
